Understanding Host-based Access Control (HBAC)
Host-based Access Control (HBAC) is a security mechanism that controls which users can access which services on which hosts within a network. In FreeIPA, HBAC rules define:

Who can access (users/groups)
What they can access (services)
Where they can access from (hosts/hostgroups)
When they can access (time-based restrictions)
Key HBAC Components
HBAC Rules: Policies that define access permissions
Users/Groups: Entities that require access
Hosts/Host Groups: Target systems for access
Services: Specific services like SSH, sudo, or custom applications
Task 1: Creating HBAC Rules
Subtask 1.1: Connect to FreeIPA Server
First, establish connection to your FreeIPA server and authenticate as administrator.

# Connect to FreeIPA server
ssh admin@ipa-server.example.com

# Authenticate with Kerberos
kinit admin
Enter the admin password when prompted.

Subtask 1.2: Examine Current HBAC Rules
Before creating new rules, let's examine the existing HBAC configuration.

# List all existing HBAC rules
ipa hbacrule-find

# Show details of the default allow_all rule
ipa hbacrule-show allow_all
Expected Output Analysis:

The allow_all rule typically grants broad access
This rule should be disabled in production environments for security
Subtask 1.3: Create Basic HBAC Rule
Create your first HBAC rule to control SSH access for developers.

# Create HBAC rule for developers
ipa hbacrule-add dev_ssh_access \
  --desc="Allow developers SSH access to development servers"

# Verify the rule was created
ipa hbacrule-show dev_ssh_access
Subtask 1.4: Create Additional HBAC Rules
Create more specific rules for different scenarios.

# Create rule for database administrators
ipa hbacrule-add dba_server_access \
  --desc="Database administrators access to DB servers"

# Create rule for web administrators
ipa hbacrule-add web_admin_access \
  --desc="Web administrators access to web servers"

# Create restrictive rule for contractors
ipa hbacrule-add contractor_limited_access \
  --desc="Limited access for contractor accounts"
Subtask 1.5: Configure Rule Services
Add specific services to your HBAC rules.

# Add SSH service to developer rule
ipa hbacrule-add-service dev_ssh_access --hbacsvcs=sshd

# Add multiple services to DBA rule
ipa hbacrule-add-service dba_server_access --hbacsvcs=sshd
ipa hbacrule-add-service dba_server_access --hbacsvcs=sudo

# Verify service assignments
ipa hbacrule-show dev_ssh_access --all
Task 2: Assigning HBAC Rules to Users and Groups
Subtask 2.1: Create Test Users and Groups
First, create users and groups for testing HBAC functionality.

# Create user groups
ipa group-add developers --desc="Development team members"
ipa group-add dbadmins --desc="Database administrators"
ipa group-add contractors --desc="External contractors"

# Create test users
ipa user-add jdeveloper \
  --first=John \
  --last=Developer \
  --password

ipa user-add msmith \
  --first=Mary \
  --last=Smith \
  --password

ipa user-add ccontractor \
  --first=Chris \
  --last=Contractor \
  --password
Note: You'll be prompted to set initial passwords for each user.

Subtask 2.2: Add Users to Groups
Assign users to appropriate groups.

# Add users to groups
ipa group-add-member developers --users=jdeveloper
ipa group-add-member dbadmins --users=msmith
ipa group-add-member contractors --users=ccontractor

# Verify group memberships
ipa group-show developers
ipa group-show dbadmins
ipa group-show contractors
Subtask 2.3: Create Host Groups
Organize hosts into logical groups for easier management.

# Create host groups
ipa hostgroup-add dev-servers --desc="Development servers"
ipa hostgroup-add db-servers --desc="Database servers"
ipa hostgroup-add web-servers --desc="Web servers"

# Add hosts to groups (replace with your actual hostnames)
ipa hostgroup-add-member dev-servers --hosts=client1.example.com
ipa hostgroup-add-member db-servers --hosts=client2.example.com
ipa hostgroup-add-member web-servers --hosts=client1.example.com
Subtask 2.4: Assign Users/Groups to HBAC Rules
Connect users and groups to the appropriate HBAC rules.

# Assign developers group to dev_ssh_access rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user dev_ssh_access --groups=developers

# Assign DBA group to database access rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user dba_server_access --groups=dbadmins

# Assign contractor user to limited access rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user contractor_limited_access --users=ccontractor

# Verify user assignments
ipa hbacrule-show dev_ssh_access --all
ipa hbacrule-show dba_server_access --all
Subtask 2.5: Assign Host Groups to HBAC Rules
Define which hosts each rule applies to.

# Assign host groups to rules
ipa hbacrule-add-host dev_ssh_access --hostgroups=dev-servers
ipa hbacrule-add-host dba_server_access --hostgroups=db-servers
ipa hbacrule-add-host contractor_limited_access --hostgroups=dev-servers

# Add specific host restrictions for contractors
ipa hbacrule-add-host contractor_limited_access --hosts=client1.example.com

# Verify host assignments
ipa hbacrule-show dev_ssh_access --all
Subtask 2.6: Disable Default Allow All Rule
For security, disable the permissive default rule.

# Disable the allow_all rule
ipa hbacrule-disable allow_all

# Verify the rule is disabled
ipa hbacrule-show allow_all
Important: Only disable this rule after ensuring your specific rules are properly configured, or you may lock yourself out.

