Why This Matters
Host-based Access Control is a fundamental security mechanism in enterprise environments because it:

Reduces Attack Surface: Limits where users can authenticate, reducing potential entry points for attackers
Enforces Compliance: Helps organizations meet regulatory requirements for access control and audit trails
Enables Zero Trust: Supports zero-trust security models by requiring explicit permission for every access attempt
Simplifies Management: Provides centralized control over access policies across the entire infrastructure
Improves Incident Response: Makes it easier to quickly revoke access during security incidents
